WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:04.019
Finally, particulate matter, or PM.

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:08.570
PM is just what it sounds like-- tiny particles that fall so slowly

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:11.460
they can hang around the atmosphere for a long time

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.710
before being deposited on the ground.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.200
The particles most dangerous to human health

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.120
have sizes less than a few microns.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.270
The most common measure of PM concentration in air

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:28.710
is PM 2.5, the total mass of particles less than 2.5 microns per volume

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:29.430
of air.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.000
PM can range from unburdened nanoparticles

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.860
of carbon emitted by diesel engines to tiny droplets of sulfuric acid form

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.750
formed from SOx emissions.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:42.810
PM is therefore both a primary and a secondary air pollutant.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.490
Some particles are emitted directly into the atmosphere

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:48.550
while other particles are formed in the atmosphere.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.370
PM is a major threat to human health.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:55.060
Scientists understanding the risks of PM and of other air pollutants

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.220
is mostly based on epidemiological studies.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.270
Because estimates of health risks are so important in driving decisions about

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:06.300
our energy future, it's worth taking a few minutes to look at these

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.910
epidemiological studies so that you can form your own opinions about what they

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:11.650
can and cannot tell us.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:15.090
Perhaps the simplest science that particulate matter can harm our health

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:17.810
are the correlations between measured PM levels

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.840
and hospital admissions or mortality.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.170
This data shows the impact of very high levels of PM

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.730
but it doesn't show the impact of chronic low-level exposure to PM.

00:01:27.730 --> 00:01:31.000
To figure that out, we can look at long-term health

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.310
of people living in areas with different amounts of PM pollution,

00:01:34.310 --> 00:01:36.389
for example different cities.

00:01:36.389 --> 00:01:38.180
Of course the problem is that cities differ

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:42.110
in all sorts of ways that can affect health-- other pollutants, employment,

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.200
average age, et cetera.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.320
Epidemiologists do their best to separate out each of these factors

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:51.250
and estimate the impact due to changes in PM alone.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:54.430
Here are results from one of the most influential air pollution

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:56.930
epidemiological studies ever done.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:02.300
The net result is that an increase in PM 2.5 of 10 micrograms per cubic meter

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.930
decreases your lifespan by roughly 3/4 of a year.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:12.070
The annual average PM 2.5 values in Beijing approach 100.

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:15.730
That means that residents' lives will be shortened by many years.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.350
Look up the PM levels in the city you live in

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:22.010
and figure out how much it is likely to shorten your life.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.600
How certain are these mortality estimates?

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.340
My view is that the overall estimates are pretty accurate, though they still

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.280
might easily have uncertainties of a factor of two or even more.

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:37.100
However, is much less clear which component of PM causes the problem.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:41.260
Some researchers think it's mostly due to metals and toxins,

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.465
while others think that most of the damage

00:02:43.465 --> 00:02:46.820
is simply caused by the total amount of PM which mostly

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:48.890
comes from sulfates and nitrates.

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:52.350
The answer really matters, as it should shape where people put

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.400
their efforts into pollution control.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.360
Here's the recent history of PM levels in the United States.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:03.650
Average PM 2.5 has fallen by about a third since the year 2000.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:08.490
Now have a look at the time history of all six of the US criteria pollutants.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.200
Notice that progress for ozone and PM, the things

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.260
that actually have the most health impact,

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:15.480
have been noticeably smaller than progress

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.290
for the individual primary pollutants like lead or sulfur.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.330
I do not think this is chance.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:26.210
It's easier to control single pollutants than it is to control PM and ozone,

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:29.390
where many different sources-- both natural and human--

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:33.130
contribute in complicated ways to the total pollution load.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:36.360
How have these reductions in pollution been achieved?

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.450
In almost all cases they've been achieved by technological changes

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:45.210
in the way energy is produced or used, either by changing the process

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.090
to reduce the production of the pollutant--

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:51.810
as when lead is removed from gasoline-- or by adding cleanup technologies,

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.740
like sulfur scrubbers on power plants or catalytic converters

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:57.875
on cars that remove the pollutants before they

00:03:57.875 --> 00:03:59.800
are released to the atmosphere.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.730
Beginning in California in the late 1950s,

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:06.610
the United States began the modern era of air pollution control.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:10.490
The United States Clean Air Act is arguably the single most important

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:13.740
example of air pollution regulation worldwide.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.260
The cost of control was not cheap.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:19.300
At the peak of implementation, the cost of the Clean Air Act

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:22.390
approached 1% of the US economy.

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:26.990
These costs exceeded the costs of all other environmental cleanup measures.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:28.520
What about the benefits?

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.180
The benefits were huge.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.760
Several retrospective studies suggest that the monetary benefits

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:37.570
of the Clean Air Act-- lower health care costs, longer productive lives--

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:41.020
were many times larger than the cost of implementation.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.700
By the time it has been completely implemented,

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.900
the Clean Air Act will probably extend the life of an average US citizen

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:48.950
by roughly a year.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:52.130
That's the level of impact we're talking about for environmental controls--

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:57.050
an improvement in economy, and lifespan, and quality of life for everyone.

